\section{The five statistics}{
The names are \code{amen::gofstats()}'s. Which part of the model is supposed to
account for each one is worth knowing before reading the table, because a
statistic no term addresses is not evidence against the terms you fitted:

\describe{
\item{\code{sd.rowmean}}{Spread of out-degree: how unequally actors send.
Sender effects are what account for this.}
\item{\code{sd.colmean}}{Spread of in-degree: how unequally actors are chosen.
Receiver effects account for this.}
\item{\code{dyad.dep}}{Dyadic dependence: reciprocation beyond what the
covariates imply. \code{prior_reciprocity()} and the fitted dyadic
correlation account for this.}
\item{\code{cycle.dep}}{Cyclic triads — generalized exchange. \strong{Nothing in an
additive model accounts for this}; it is what \code{R > 0} is for.}
\item{\code{trans.dep}}{Transitive triads — closure and clustering. Again
nothing additive accounts for it.}
}

So an additive fit (\code{R = 0}) reproducing the two degree statistics and
missing the two triadic ones is the expected result rather than a defect,
and it is the reason to consider multiplicative effects. Whether raising \code{R}
actually recovers them is a question for this function, run twice.
}

\section{Reading the columns}{
\code{ppp} is the posterior predictive probability, the share of simulated
networks whose statistic was at least as large as the observed one. It is
\strong{one-sided by construction}, so both ends are misfit: near 0 the observed
network is more extreme than almost every replicate, near 1 less extreme
than almost every replicate, and near 0.5 the model reproduces the feature.
Its granularity is \code{1 / n_draws}, so a short chain cannot distinguish 0 from
0.02, and no threshold is applied to it here or anywhere else.

\code{z} is the observed value's distance from the predictive mean in predictive
standard deviations. It is comparable across statistics, which \code{ppp} is not
once \code{ppp} saturates at 0 or 1, and it \strong{assumes a roughly symmetric
predictive distribution}, which \code{dyad.dep} in particular need not have.
Read \code{ppp} first and \code{z} as the tie-breaker when \code{ppp} is 0 or 1.
\code{predicted_lower} and \code{predicted_upper} are the 2.5\% and 97.5\% quantiles of
the predictive draws, which make no symmetry assumption at all.
}

\section{Refusals rather than approximations}{
A hierarchical fit (\code{pooling = selection_pooling()}) has no posterior
predictive draws and is refused. The native sampler behind that route does
not simulate a network per scan, deliberately — the simulation does not
affect the posterior. Fit the groups separately to check them.

A fit run with \code{gof = FALSE} kept the observed statistics and no draws, and
is refused rather than compared against an empty distribution. So is a fit
saved by a version of rnetis that did not retain them.
}

